I'd describe the common ground as rhythm-centred instrumental arrangement, with an ostinato-based approach — an ostinato being a recurring rhythmic or melodic figure that anchors the music while other parts evolve around it.

That is a useful musical concept for comparing these recordings without claiming they share the same Latin rhythm. In Crimson, the pattern becomes an expansive sound world; in Wilkerson, it becomes a groove you can almost physically move to.











