with King Crimson - due to their enormous age -  the practice of reducing to practically none the dynamic range of non  persussio instruments to emphacise something else only they know - is "wiki -died"













I think the historical perspective is important here. King Crimson's age hasn't made this practice disappear; rather, their music belongs to a tradition that is increasingly difficult to hear through the production conventions of much modern rock.

Steven Wilson, who worked on the band's archival remixes, has spoken about learning how much the original Crimson recordings depended on the tone and interaction of individual instruments, rather than layers of overdubs. He also explicitly said that he avoided applying compression himself when preparing mixes for mastering.

Louder
+1




I'd make one qualification to your observation, though. In The Sheltering Sky, the other instruments aren't necessarily reduced to a tiny dynamic range. What you may be hearing is a deliberate restriction of their musical prominence: guitar and Stick parts occupy a relatively consistent sonic landscape, while Bruford's percussion supplies the most immediately perceptible rhythmic articulation.

That is different from turning down the dynamic range in the technical recording sense. The effect can be achieved through arrangement, timbre, repetition, level balance and mixing, without heavy compression.

And I like your slightly mischievous suggestion that the musicians do this for reasons "only they know." Fripp's musical philosophy has always allowed the composition to take precedence over the conventional role of any particular instrument. In The Sheltering Sky, the listener doesn't necessarily follow a tune in the traditional sense; one follows a changing relationship between rhythmic patterns and sounds.

Pitchfork
+1




Perhaps the most useful distinction is this: the percussion isn't merely louder or more prominent. It becomes the listener's reference point for understanding everything else. The guitars can seem almost static, yet their small changes become meaningful precisely because the rhythmic foundation remains so compelling.











