with latin genres many of which put drum as a centeral performing instrument while brass or piano playing *the same * note through the whole piece is k/c - alike













Yes — and in Afro-Cuban music, the similarity to King Crimson is more than a superficial resemblance. There is a shared musical principle: a repeated pattern can become the organizing structure of the whole performance, while other instruments contribute to that structure rather than continually developing a conventional melody.

The technical terms help make your comparison precise.
- Ostinato: a recurring musical pattern.
- Montuno or guajeo: a repeated, syncopated piano figure characteristic of Cuban-derived music.
- Interlocking rhythms: distinct instrumental patterns that fit together to create a larger rhythmic design.
In Afro-Cuban ensembles, even the piano and bass have important rhythmic responsibilities. Their repeated patterns interact with the percussion and with the underlying clave, producing a composite groove.



There is a particularly interesting parallel in The Sheltering Sky: Bruford's log drum supplies a distinctive rhythmic foundation, while Levin's Chapman Stick and the two guitarists contribute their own recurring patterns and textures.

John McFerrin Music Reviews




The crucial distinction is that the Latin ensemble's repeated piano or brass figure is not necessarily musically static. It can be harmonically simple yet rhythmically sophisticated. A horn section may repeat the same note or short phrase, but its articulation and placement against the percussion give it life.

